{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-76345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76355" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76355" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76355" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-165414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76610" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3-{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76610" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-165413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] ["math" "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-165413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2y+3x)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2y+3x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+3)-(-2+0) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76658" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\pi -1"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76658" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-41188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-41188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-41188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-41188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-41188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "8\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-41188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76616" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta | \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ because $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76688" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76688" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the polar curve $r=3$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3({e}^{3}-1)"] ["math" "3({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)"] ["math" "3{e}^{\\pi /2}"] ["math" "9(e-1)"] ["math" "9({e}^{2}-1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3({e}^{3}-1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{3}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=6$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =3({(3\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "243"] ["math" "27"] ["math" "81"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "81"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}8{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =16{\\int }_{1}^{1/2}-{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}{|}_{1}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{6}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(-\\frac{63}{64}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76749" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{15}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{10}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76749" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{21}{8}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\theta  & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}-0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76767" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "73f1c4689759025eda6a8fa7ba26adc18bd923797311aa204568cc0afcf0ae98"} {"question" "q-76767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi +1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "q-76767" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc6faefd06e7d0d5aa16aac4852135156de5edef1c47ef029011b648be1365e3"} {"question" "e-6313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{ \\pi}4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/example-6313.json" "file_sha256" "bd13abc4cd70ebfd64379108ece74340dea04e782ce64837425ee04acd71ea21"} {"question" "e-6313" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/example-6313.json" "file_sha256" "bd13abc4cd70ebfd64379108ece74340dea04e782ce64837425ee04acd71ea21"} {"question" "e-6314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/example-6314.json" "file_sha256" "a8a20eb43b8c89061ef5ec75633571b2c036c1d8b01659886b9e3126e009a911"} {"question" "e-6314" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/example-6314.json" "file_sha256" "a8a20eb43b8c89061ef5ec75633571b2c036c1d8b01659886b9e3126e009a911"} {"question" "e-6315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/example-6315.json" "file_sha256" "a95bfe72a9410609a870f0f0f713080b572cac4704bba0c142ce99b3d52390ec"} {"question" "e-6315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079318/example-6315.json" "file_sha256" "a95bfe72a9410609a870f0f0f713080b572cac4704bba0c142ce99b3d52390ec"}] "usage" {"e-6315" [] "e-6313" [] "q-41188" [] "q-76345" [] "q-76355" [] "e-6314" [] "q-76347" []} "basis" 2819 "retractions" [[17592186152661 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)(x^{2} + y^{2})ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $0 \\le θ \\le π$?"] [17592186152664 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)x^{2}yds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{4}$?"] [17592186152516 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $∫_(C)(x + y)ds$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r = 2\\sqrt{\\cos 2θ}$ for $-\\frac{π}{4} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{4}$."] [17592186152662 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the polar curve $r = 3$ for $0 \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{2}$."] [17592186151874 :question/problem "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds$, where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{\\pi}4$."] [17592186151874 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =y^2-x\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= (\\cos θ\\sin θ)^{2} - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ\\sin^{2} θ - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ(\\sin^{2} θ - 1) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ(-\\cos^{2} θ) \\\\\n&=-\\cos^{4} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(y^{2} - x)ds &= ∫_{0}^{π/4}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/4} - \\cos^{4} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{4} θdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151875 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2$."] [17592186151875 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ = \\cos θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ = \\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\frac{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= \\csc^{2} θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} &= \\frac{d}{dθ}(1) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ = \\sqrt{1^{2} + 0^{2}} = 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{y^{2}}ds &= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θ \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/6}^{π/2}\\csc^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&=-\\cot θ \\mid _{π/6}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= - (0 - \\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186151876 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$."] [17592186151876 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,\n$$\n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= \\cos^{2} θ, \\\\\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ \\cdot \\sin θ \\\\\n&= \\cos θ\\sin θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos^2\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\n, where\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, if\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ) &= \\cos^{2} θ \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin θ(1 - (\\cos^{2} θ)^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dr}{dθ} = \\frac{d}{dθ}(\\cos θ) =-\\sin θ\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ &= \\sqrt{(\\cos θ)^{2} + (-\\sin θ)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)xy(1 - x^{2})ds &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}f(r\\cos θ, r\\sin θ)\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ) \\cdot 1 \\cdot dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} θ\\sin θ(1 - \\cos^{4} θ)dθ &= ∫_{1}^{0} - u^{3}(1 - u^{4})du \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(u^{3} - u^{7})du \\\\\n&= [\\frac{u^{4}}{4} - \\frac{u^{8}}{8}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{8}) - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186150007 17592186150008 17592186150009 17592186150010 17592186150011 17592186150877 17592186150878 17592186150879 17592186150880 17592186150881 17592186150883 17592186150884 17592186150885 17592186150886 17592186150887 17592186150895 17592186150896 17592186150897 17592186150898 17592186150899] "immutable_fields" [[17592186150006 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150006 :answer-field/id] [17592186150006 :answer-field/key] [17592186150006 :answer-field/type] [17592186150876 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150876 :answer-field/id] [17592186150876 :answer-field/key] [17592186150876 :answer-field/type] [17592186150882 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150882 :answer-field/id] [17592186150882 :answer-field/key] [17592186150882 :answer-field/type] [17592186150894 :answer-field/correct] [17592186150894 :answer-field/id] [17592186150894 :answer-field/key] [17592186150894 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
